#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203699 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7ca5b3a-0cd7-5325-8b14-6ac2b0dda994" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "ac47763a-fdd2-50db-a6e0-28bb60d68650" :question/math-academy-id "e-14652" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A = \\{2,5,9 \\}$.\n$R = \\big\\{(2,2), (2,5), (2,9), (5,9), (9,9) \\big\\}$\nDetermine whether the relation is symmetric, antisymmetric, both, or neither." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y$.\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x)$, can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x)$.\n\nOur relation is defined on $\\{2,5,9 \\}$. Notice that:\n\n- $(2,5) \\in R$ and $(5,2) \\notin R$\n- $(2,9) \\in R$ and $(9,2) \\notin R$\n- $(5,9) \\in R$ and $(9,5) \\notin R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nin the relation. So, for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is antisymmetric."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203700 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "2939591d-d7e6-52bb-9ed6-1823a1a66139" :question/math-academy-id "e-14653" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c7734813ff7d452ee1a8eccb669ef287eb41e6de3e1abe83a58de98c8316fbd.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a relation, defined on the set $A = \\{p,q,r\\}$, that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x \\rightarrow y$, then the arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ must be in the digraph too.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y$.\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x$, can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x \\rightarrow y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y \\rightarrow x$ also belongs to the graph.\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x \\neq y$, *at most one* of the arrows (either $x \\rightarrow y$ or $y \\rightarrow x$, but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $p \\rightarrow q$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $q \\rightarrow p$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $p \\rightarrow r$ and $r \\rightarrow p$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203701 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "928152e5-ebf4-5a63-9b5d-4a6da031bf34" :question/math-academy-id "e-14654" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric?\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\leq y$, defined on $\\mathbb{R}$\n- $x \\: S \\: y$ if and only if $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$, defined on $\\mathbb{Z}$\n- $X \\: T \\: Y$ if and only if $X \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N}$, defined on $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y$.\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x)$, can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that $(0,1) \\in R$ since $0 \\leq 1$, while $(1,0) \\notin R$ since $1 \\not \\leq 0$.\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y) \\in S$ then $(y,x) \\in S$. Notice that\n$x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y \\equiv x \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$\nfor all $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y) \\in T$ then $(Y,X) \\in T$. Notice that\n$X \\cap Y = \\mathbb{N} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad Y \\cap X = \\mathbb{N}$\nfor all $X, Y \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203706 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f60a9114-56fc-5360-ae43-4601b4dee096" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From Adjacency Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "0b044d86-9aee-521e-ae87-62db34b98e5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14664" :question/problem "Consider a relation $R$ defined on the set $A = \\{1, 4, 9\\}$. Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the corresponding adjacency matrix below.\n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *symmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad (y,x) \\in R$.\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n- A binary relation $R$, defined on a set $A$, is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y \\in A$, we have\n$(x,y) \\in R \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (y,x) \\in R \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=y$.\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x)$, can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x)$.\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the main diagonal:\n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pairs $(9,1)$ and $(9,4)$. Since $R$ is be symmetric, we require\n\n$$\n(1,9) \\in R, \\qquad (4,9) \\in R\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all equal $1$.\n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\phantom{1}}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAlso, note the following:\n\n- The symmetric relation condition is equivalent to $(x,y)\\notin R \\Longrightarrow (y,x) \\notin R$. Therefore, $(4,1)\\notin R$ implies $(1,4) \\notin R$.\n- We do not have enough information to determine whether $(1,1)$ is an element of $R$.\n\nLet's add this information to our table.\n\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}?}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7ca5b3a-0cd7-5325-8b14-6ac2b0dda994" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6ebae6-27a8-57ca-a9a4-72fdbf9d400d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200236} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8099c2-c7ab-585d-90b4-21035483aeb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200237}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cf274dd9-4548-5d01-96e6-48067ded4280" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8e13ea9-7f3d-5423-9f2e-26d02676eec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 0) ∉ R" :db/